","summary":"Added `ProblemCode.OVER_LOW_PRIORITY_CAP` (appended last) with a matching `Problem.overLowPriorityCap(inputIndex, requestId, cap)` factory, and taught `WorkRequestSorter` an optional per-batch Low cap: it sorts first, keeps the earliest `cap` LOW requests (ties by requestId, the order the stage already uses), reports the rest as problems ordered by input index, and emits sorted "`<id>` dropped: over Low-priority cap of `<cap>`" notes while shrinking the prioritize trace's `out`. `BatchProperties` gained a second component `maxLowPriority` with `@DefaultValue("1000")` and a ">= 0" check, keeping the one-arg constructor delegating to 1000, and `ProcessingConfig` now builds the sorter from `BatchProperties.maxLowPriority()`; `BatchObserver` needed no change since it already registers one counter per `ProblemCode` value.","uncertainties":["I read 'notes exactly ["L-2 dropped...", "L-3 dropped..."]' literally: when any request is over cap the prioritize notes are only the cap notes, replacing the usual "Sorted by priority ..." note; when nothing is over cap the original note is unchanged (so existing no-cap tests keep passing).","I was not shown WorkRequestProcessor, so I assume it already aggregates each stage's StageResult.problems() in stage order; if it ignored the sorter's problems, cap problems would not surface and the processor would need a small change.","OrderIndependenceTest.generatedBatchCoversEveryKindOfProblem asserts every ProblemCode appears in a generated batch; since I could not see that file I left it untouched, assuming the criteria's own tests update the generator (it needs a LOW request over a configured cap).","WorkRequestSorter(int) rejects negatives with "maxLowPriority must be at least 0, was -1"; the criteria only pin that message for BatchProperties, so the sorter's wording is my choice.","I did not add app.batch.max-low-priority to application.yml so the binding test sees a genuinely unconfigured property defaulting to 1000."]}

